% Flip queries (LE_extensions_proposal §3.7), on the housing benefit rules of
% rkBook/housing_benefit.le (Kowalski, Computational Logic and Human Thinking,
% §5.7).
%
% "which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that <goal>" asks
% for the smallest sets of scenario changes — adding or removing facts of the
% scenario-element templates, never of derived ones — after which the goal
% holds (or, for "it is not the case that ...", no longer holds). Each answer
% comes with the proof the changed scenario gives. "expects changes" lists the
% expected minimal sets.
%
% - rich works part-time but is not on a low income, so is ineligible: the one
%   minimal change is to add that he is on a low income. (Adding "on other
%   benefits" or "works full-time on a low income" would not do: he would
%   still be ineligible.)
% - bob gets help through other benefits, on a low income: removing either
%   fact takes the help away; no single fact needs adding, and no two-fact
%   set is minimal.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a person* gets help to pay rent.
    *a person* receives housing benefit.
    *a person* is on other benefits; undefined.
    *a person* works part-time; undefined.
    *a person* works full-time on a low income; undefined.
    *a person* is on a low income; undefined.
    *a person* is ineligible to receive housing benefit.

the knowledge base flip housing includes:

a person gets help to pay rent
    if the person receives housing benefit.

a person receives housing benefit
    if the person is on other benefits
    and it is not the case that
        the person is ineligible to receive housing benefit.

a person receives housing benefit
    if the person works part-time
    and it is not the case that
        the person is ineligible to receive housing benefit.

a person receives housing benefit
    if the person works full-time on a low income
    and it is not the case that
        the person is ineligible to receive housing benefit.

a person is ineligible to receive housing benefit
    if it is not the case that
        the person is on a low income.

scenario rich is:
    rich works part-time.
    flip_rich expects changes [["add: rich is on a low income"]].

scenario bob is:
    bob is on other benefits.
    bob is on a low income.
    flip_bob expects changes [["remove: bob is on a low income"], ["remove: bob is on other benefits"]].

query flip_rich is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        rich gets help to pay rent.

query flip_bob is:
    which minimal change to the scenario makes it the case that
        it is not the case that bob gets help to pay rent.

% A view (docs/le_summary.md §17.10): the rules as a citizen's check on a
% phone — four questions, asked only when the answer still depends on them,
% and the answer in the view's words.
the view benefit check is:
    the title is "Can I get help to pay my rent?".
    the case is about the applicant.
    the facts are asked one at a time.
    the question for the applicant is on other benefits is "Do you receive other benefits?".
    the question for the applicant works part-time is "Do you work part-time?".
    the question for the applicant works full-time on a low income is "Do you work full-time on a low income?".
    the question for the applicant is on a low income is "Is your income low?".
    the result is whether the applicant gets help to pay rent.
    the result reads "You can get help to pay your rent." when it holds.
    the result reads "You cannot get help to pay your rent, on what you told us." when it does not.
    the result shows its reasons.
    the result can be flipped, as "What would change this?".
